  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gusty conditions continue through Saturday and \nrain chances return to the coast as a nor'easter strengthens over \nthe weekend. \n\nThere is now good model consensus regarding the track of the \nstrengthening nor'easter off the coast. Most of the guidance is \nholding fast to pivoting the low into New Jersey later this \nweekend, then the low wobbles around the Mid- Atlantic before \ntracking up the coast into New England early next week. Thus, \nconfidence high for increased impacts locally, especially for \nthe Eastern Shore. This shift has resulted in an increase in \nwinds and rain chances tonight through Saturday, particularly \nfor the coast. Wind gusts of 45 to 50mph will be possible along \nimmediate coastal areas with gusts up to 30mph away from the \nimmediate coast and up to 25mph for interior portions of the \narea. A Wind Advisory has been issued for Worcester, Accomack, \nand Northampton counties and is in effect this afternoon through\nmid morning Saturday. Winds will gradually diminish Saturday \nnight through Sunday, but remaining breezy with gusts up to \n30mph expected at the coast. Lighter winds finally return Monday\nas the storm pulls northeast and high pressure builds in. Rain \nwill push back into coastal areas from offshore later tonight \nand especially tomorrow as the low slides north and west. The \nsteadier rain will likely remain on the Eastern Shore where QPF \nis between 0.5-1.5\". For other coastal areas, the rain should \nstay on the lighter side closer to drizzle with QPF under 0.25\".\nRain/drizzle could push as far west as the piedmont/I-95 \ncorridor Saturday afternoon. Rain may linger in the far \nnortheast on Sunday, but otherwise drying out. The adjusted \nnor'easter track also has an impact on coastal flooding, which \nis addressed in key message 2. \n\nHigh pressure takes over next week leading to calmer weather. \nLooking at dry conditions and temperatures warming back into \nthe 80s through the week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread moderate to major tidal flooding \n(inundation of 2-3+ feet AGL in vulnerable areas) will continue into \nSaturday night or early Sunday morning at most spots, with some \nlocations especially in the middle Bay seeing flooding persist \nthrough the weekend.\n\nAs a Nor'easter continues to develop off the coast, winds have \ncontinued to back over the region, now closer to N to NNE. In \naddition, the speeds have also decreased slightly at 20-30 kt with \ngusts to 35 kt. However, this has done little to change the coastal \nflood situation with most sites still seeing anomalies of 2.5-3.5 ft \nabove normal astronomical tide. With little change overnight through \nSaturday, expect similar anomalies to persist. These anomalies \ncombined with higher astronomical tides will likely will allow for \ncontinued rounds of Moderate to Major tidal flooding across a \nmajority of the tidal sites through Saturday.\n\nOverall, storm surge guidance has trended even higher with the 12z \nruns, especially inside the Chesapeake Bay, for overnight and \nSaturday. This is due to the models trending west and slightly \nstronger with the Nor'easter. This will lead to a more N-NW flow \ninside the bay leading to more ebbing at the mouth of the Bay, it is \nalso possible that especially the eastern and southern shores of the \nChesapeake Bay could see increased water levels with a better \nonshore component to the winds. The tide forecast has been raised \nslightly for most sides, with the most substantial changes (~0.5 ft) \nacross aforementioned areas. Coastal Flood Warnings were extended out \nanother cycle, Saturday night-Sunday morning, and additional \nextensions are likely, especially across the middle Bay.",
